(The after-`Free` contract: length 0, `as text` "", a byte read past length
 sets the error flag `On error` catches, and every write - a byte read is
 fine to attempt, an append or a copy is not - is refused and sets the flag
 too, without ever touching the buffer again. Before this fix none of the
 three flags fired: the buffer was never actually freed, so byte 1, append,
 and copy all still landed on live memory.)

a buffer called line is "hello".
Free line.
Print "length {line's length}".
Print "text [{line as text}]".

a number called b is byte 1 of line.
On error Print "byte read flagged".

append "z" to line.
On error Print "append flagged".

Copy "more words" to line.
On error Print "copy flagged".

Print "end".
